# ESLint — strict type-checked TypeScript

**Source:** `packages/config/eslint-config/base.js` extends `typescript-eslint` **`strictTypeChecked`** (all apps/packages with typed ESLint). React apps add `react.js` + `ui-only.js` where configured.

**Workflow:** After each batch of files in a package, run **`pnpm lint`** in that package (or root turbo), not only at the end. Forbidden fixes: `eslint-disable`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error`, `as any`, non-null `!` to silence lint.

## Rules that fail often (write correct code the first time)

### `@typescript-eslint/restrict-template-expressions`

Template literals may only embed types ESLint allows (typically **`string`**; **`number` is not allowed** in this preset).

```typescript
// BAD
style={{ width: `${Math.min(100, progress)}%` }}

// GOOD — coerce before the template
const widthPct = String(Math.min(100, Math.max(0, progress)));
style={{ width: `${widthPct}%` }}
```

Same for `` `${count} items` ``, `` `id-${id}` `` when `id` is number: use `String(id)` or `.toString()`.

### `@typescript-eslint/no-unnecessary-condition`

Do not re-check null/undefined after TypeScript (or a prior guard) already narrowed the type.

```typescript
// BAD — showProgress already implies progress was narrowed
const showProgress = isUploading && progress != null;
const w = showProgress && progress != null ? String(progress) : '0';

// GOOD — one derived value, one guard
const progressPercent =
  typeof progress === 'number' ? Math.min(100, Math.max(0, progress)) : null;
const showProgress = isUploading && progressPercent !== null;
const widthPct = progressPercent === null ? '0' : String(progressPercent);
```

Avoid `if (x)` when `x` is always truthy/falsy per types. Remove dead branches instead of disabling the rule.

### `@typescript-eslint/no-confusing-void-expression`

Do not use `() => doSomething()` when `doSomething()` returns `void` — ESLint treats that as returning a void expression. Use a block:

```typescript
// BAD
onClick={() => onOpenChange(false)}

// GOOD
onClick={() => {
  onOpenChange(false);
}}
```

### `@typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises`

Await promises, or **`void`** only for intentional fire-and-forget (e.g. `void navigate('/x')`, `void refetch()` in a click handler). Never leave a bare `mutation()` or `fetch()` call in an handler without `void`/await.

### `@typescript-eslint/no-invalid-void-type`

RTK Query endpoints with **no argument** use **`undefined`**, not **`void`**: `builder.query<Result, undefined>({...})`, call with `useQuery(undefined)`.

### Imports and hygiene

- **`import type`** for type-only imports.
- No unused vars/imports (`@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars` with `_` prefix allowed).
- No `console.*` in app code (api: Nest logger; web/site: none).

### `@app/ui` extra

`packages/ui` also runs **`node scripts/check-logical-classes.mjs`** — use logical Tailwind (`ms-*`, `pe-*`, `text-start`), not physical `ml-*`/`mr-*`/`text-left`.

## UI-only `.tsx` (apps/web, apps/site)

See `45-components-ui-only.mdc` and `ui-only.js`: every feature `.tsx` (`src/features/**/*.tsx`) must not import `useState`, RTK Query, etc. Logic lives only in `.ts` files (`hooks/*.ts`, `*.utils.ts`). Every feature `.tsx` is UI only; never move logic to a new folder to get around this.

## Backend (apps/api, packages/shared): mistakes seen in this repo

These came up again and again in the order-model work. Write them correctly the first time.

### `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`: `rows[0]` is `T | undefined`

```typescript
// BAD: type error, or a `!` to silence it
const row = (await tx.select().from(orders).where(eq(orders.id, id)).limit(1))[0]!;

// GOOD: return `T | undefined` and let the service throw NotFoundError,
// or use the repository's firstRow() helper after an INSERT ... RETURNING
const rows = await tx.select().from(orders).where(eq(orders.id, id)).limit(1);
return rows[0];
```

### Raw SQL results are untyped (`no-unsafe-member-access`, `no-unsafe-assignment`)

Prefer the Drizzle query builder. When raw `tx.execute(sql`...`)` is needed (upserts with RETURNING,
`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`), describe the row once and check it:

```typescript
// BAD
const n = result.rows[0].last_order_number;

// GOOD
const row = result.rows[0] as { last_order_number: number } | undefined;
if (!row) {
  throw new Error('Counter increment failed');
}
return row.last_order_number;
```

### Numbers and dates in template strings (`restrict-template-expressions`)

```typescript
// BAD
`${year}-${month}`        `Cannot void item ${index}`        `${createdAt}`
// GOOD
`${String(year)}-${String(month).padStart(2, '0')}`        createdAt.toISOString()
```

### `catch` is `unknown`

```typescript
// BAD
catch (err) { if (err.code === '23505') ... }
// GOOD: narrow with the existing helper (lib/pg-errors.ts) or a type guard
catch (err: unknown) { if (isPgUniqueViolation(err, 'orders_branch_client_order_id_unique')) ... }
```

### Don't re-validate what Zod already validated (`no-unnecessary-condition`)

A DTO built from a Zod schema is already parsed. If the schema says `z.string().min(1)`, the value is a
non-empty string: don't check `if (!body.label)` again. Put conditional rules (e.g. "reason required
when cancelling") in the Zod schema with `superRefine`, or check once in the service, not both.

### Express/Nest response status

Set a dynamic status with `@Res({ passthrough: true }) res: Response` and `res.status(201)`, and still
`return` the body. Never call `res.json()` yourself (it skips the exception filter and interceptors).

## E2E tests (supertest): mistakes seen in this repo

### `withBearer()` / `authAgent()` mutate the agent you pass in

`agent.set('Authorization', ...)` changes that agent and returns the same object. Two users on one agent
means every request uses the LAST login, so tests get random 403/404s.

```typescript
// BAD: owner and other are the same object; owner now sends other's token
const owner = await asDemoOwner(agent);
const other = await asOtherOwner(agent);

// GOOD: one agent per user
const owner = await asDemoOwner(apiAgent(app));
const other = await asOtherOwner(apiAgent(app));
```

### A supertest request is a promise (`no-floating-promises`)

```typescript
// BAD: `void req.set(...)` or a bare `req.set(...)` statement
// GOOD: build the request in one expression
const base = client.post(url);
const req = key === undefined ? base : base.set('Idempotency-Key', key);
return req.send(body);
```

### Test data must be unique on every run

Local test databases are NOT reset between runs. Never use fixed names, emails, labels or dates that hit
a unique constraint or a counter:
- UUIDv7 starts with the timestamp: `newUuidV7().slice(0, 8)` repeats for about a minute. Use `.slice(-8)`.
- Fixed `Clock` dates: add a random number of whole days so each run gets an unused business day.
- Lists you reorder or count: pass `?limit=100`, or only assert on rows the test created.

### Close every Nest module you compile

`Test.createTestingModule(...).compile()` opens a DB pool and a Redis client. Reuse `app.get(...)` from
`createTestApp()`; if you must compile another module (to override a provider), close it in `afterAll`.
Otherwise Jest prints "did not exit one second after the test run has completed".

## When unsure

Run `pnpm lint` in the touched package and fix the reported rule by name — do not weaken ESLint config or add disables without asking.
